title: 'txpool: hold the transaction pool as a trait object instead of a wrapper'

doc:
  - audience: Node Dev
    description: |-
      `TransactionPoolWrapper` was a struct wrapping a boxed transaction pool, existing only to give
      it a sized type. Everything it did was forward a method to the pool inside it.
      `TransactionPoolHandle` is now a type alias for the trait object itself rather than for that
      wrapper, so the forwarding layer is gone and `Builder::build` hands back an
      `Arc<TransactionPoolHandle<Block>>` instead of a wrapper by value.

      `FullClientTransactionPool` no longer carries a `Client` type parameter. All three of its
      associated types are projections that discard the client, so `Hash`, `InPoolTransaction` and
      `Error` are spelled out in terms of `Block` alone. Naming them that way required widening the
      public API of `sc-transaction-pool`: `sc_transaction_pool::error` and
      `sc_transaction_pool::Transaction` are now exported.

      `BuildNetworkParams`, `BuildNetworkAdvancedParams`, `SpawnTasksParams` and
      `GenRpcModuleParams` keep a generic transaction pool parameter, relaxed to `?Sized` so a trait
      object can be passed in. The service therefore does not pin the pool's error or in-pool
      transaction type, and an alternative pool implementation is free to define its own. What those
      entry points no longer do is constrain the client on the transaction pool's behalf: the
      `BlockIdTo` and `TaggedTransactionQueue` bounds that only existed to build a pool are gone
      from `build_network`, `build_network_advanced`, `spawn_tasks`, `gen_rpc_module` and from the
      cumulus `build_network`.

      What those entry points do need from the client is spelled out once as the new
      `sc_service::ClientForService`, in the same shape as the existing `ClientForGrandpa` and
      `ClientForTransactionPool`, with each one adding only the few capabilities it genuinely uses
      on top.

      The client capabilities needed to actually construct a pool are spelled out once as
      `sc_transaction_pool::ClientForTransactionPool`, which is used by the transaction pool
      `Builder` and internally by `FullChainApi`, `BasicPool::new_full` and
      `ForkAwareTxPool::new_full`. It does not pin the `BlockIdTo` error, so a hand written client
      is free to choose its own.

      Components that keep the pool behind an `Arc` stay generic over the pool type and accept
      unsized pools now, which is a relaxation and needs no changes on their side.

      Migration for nodes outside this repository

      - `sc_transaction_pool::Builder::build` hands back an `Arc` now, so drop the surrounding
        `Arc::from(..)` at the construction site.
      - `TransactionPoolHandle<Block, Client>` is `TransactionPoolHandle<Block>` now.
      - Add `?Sized` to any generic parameter of your own that holds the pool behind an `Arc`,
        because `TransactionPoolHandle` is a trait object and no longer has a statically known
        size. The parameters listed above already carry it, so passing the handle into them needs
        no change.

